  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">A comprehensive social security strategy to enhance coverage to Palestinian women and men is developed in line with international labour standards</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Social protection has become a central tool to respond to lifecycle risks and threats of poverty faced by Palestinian society. In September 2023, the Palestinian Authority&#x2019;s cabinet adopted a memo to introduce individual rights-based allowances. This decision represents a major milestone in the commitment of the Palestinian Ministry of Social Development to strengthen and invest in the Palestinian national social protection system, to move towards comprehensive, adequate and inclusive social protection for all. The newly funded lifecycle social grants approach introduces a monthly allowance for older persons (above 65 years old) and a disability allowance for persons of all ages. Social allowances offer a transparent and dignifying way to provide income support, allowing groups suffering some of the highest levels of social and economic exclusion to maintain an adequate standard of living. Poor households will remain eligible to receive social assistance under the existing poverty-targeted programme funded by the same source.  
The Social Protection Cash and Voucher Assistance Thematic Working Group (SPCVA TWG) was established in 2022 to enhance coherence and alignment across humanitarian, development and government programming. It is co-chaired by a representative of the Palestinian Authority and a representative of humanitarian actors and facilitated by the ILO and the Cash Working Group. This joint leadership has increased the interaction and understanding between key actors, working towards a co-created and jointly owned roadmap and vision. 
The roll-out of social insurance schemes is essential for the long-term financial sustainability of the system, whilst moving towards social allowances and lifecycle-based transfers will help protect vulnerable groups over time. Coverage of contributory social insurance remains extremely limited in the Occupied Palestinian Territory (OPT), depriving the government of an otherwise effective tool for risk-pooling and mitigat</narrative>
  </description>
